Abstract

We present Physis, a verifiable operating system for executable science that transforms structured scientific knowledge into validated physical artifacts through a rigorously specified Capsule Intermediate Representation (CIR). The framework unifies five modular, uncertainty-aware layers—Knowledge Ingestion, Cognitive Reasoning, Differentiable Multi-Physics Simulation, Embodied Execution, and Recursive Self-Improvement—around a single canonical data structure, the Scientific Capsule. Every layer propagates distribution‑free, conformalised prediction intervals over its outputs, enabling principled decision‑making under epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty with finite‑sample coverage guarantees. A cross‑cutting five‑level Capability Tier System governs all physical actions through both static, machine‑checkable constitutional constraints encoded in Lean 4 and a dynamic, real‑time safety filter based on Control Barrier Functions, providing a layered assurance architecture that no learned component can override. A Computational Thermodynamics Layer enforces resource‑awareness by imposing hard state constraints on energy, thermal, and memory‑bandwidth coordinates within the model predictive controller. Continual learning is unified under a variational inference framework with first‑order meta‑learning, preventing catastrophic forgetting while enabling rapid adaptation across heterogeneous capsule types. We provide a complete mathematical specification of the CIR, including its typed grammar and a retrieval‑augmented autoformalisation pipeline, conformal propagation rules with adaptive gating, a formal treatment of safety via static proof and dynamic CBF shielding, and a complexity analysis of the full pipeline.

