1. Introduction

Scientific knowledge, locked in heterogeneous publications, resists direct translation into physical systems. A journal article encodes a mixture of formal mathematics, informal reasoning, geometric intuition, and tacit craft knowledge. Converting this mixture into a validated artefact requires heroic human effort, error‑prone reimplementation, and opaque design tracing. The cost scales super‑linearly with the complexity of the science.

Recent advances have produced powerful but fragmented components: large language models capable of scientific text and code synthesis, differentiable physics engines for gradient‑based design, neural theorem provers operating in formal languages, dexterous manipulators executing contact‑rich tasks, and self‑driving laboratories for materials and chemistry. Yet no coherent operating system unifies these components into an auditable, verifiable, physically grounded whole.

Physis supplies precisely this missing layer. Instead of building another monolithic agent, it defines an open, composable protocol centred on the Scientific Capsule—a machine‑verifiable package that travels from knowledge ingestion through multi‑fidelity simulation, fabrication, and recursive empirical refinement. This specification incorporates recent advances in conformal prediction, control barrier functions, variational continual learning, and symplectic differentiable physics to address fundamental limitations of earlier designs. Our contributions are as follows.

C1. A formally specified Capsule Intermediate Representation with a typed grammar, a unit‑aware type system enforcing dimensional homogeneity, a cryptographic immutability model, and a validation compiler.

C2. A mathematically rigorous uncertainty propagation framework that equips every inter‑layer quantity with distribution‑free prediction intervals via conformalised quantile regression, and gates physical execution decisions with false‑discovery‑rate‑controlled adaptive thresholds.

C3. A dual static‑dynamic safety architecture comprising (i) a Lean‑4‑based constitutional constraint system that generates machine‑checkable proof certificates for all planned actions, and (ii) a real‑time Control Barrier Function shield that projects learned controller outputs onto provably forward‑invariant safe sets.

C4. A multi‑fidelity simulation architecture with an escalation criterion grounded in Bayesian model evidence, and symplectic residual dynamics models that preserve physical invariants while closing the sim‑to‑real gap.

C5. A unified variational continual learning and first‑order meta‑learning loop that updates a probabilistic world model without catastrophic forgetting and schedules uncertainty‑driven physical micro‑experiments.

C6. A Computational Thermodynamics Layer that augments the system state with energy and thermal coordinates and enforces them as hard constraints in the model predictive controller, enabling resource‑bounded autonomous operation.

C7. A complexity analysis of the full pipeline and a phased roadmap from simulation‑only capsule validation to distributed embodied scientific ecosystems.

The remainder of this paper develops each component in full mathematical detail.

