3. Uncertainty Propagation with Conformal Calibration and Adaptive Gating

Physis enforces a foundational principle: no layer may output a point estimate; every output is a predictive density $p_k$, and every downstream layer propagates that density. However, raw ensemble approximations lack finite‑sample coverage guarantees. We therefore equip the framework with conformal prediction, providing distribution‑free prediction intervals with rigorous coverage.

3.1 Conformalised Quantile Regression for Regression KPIs

Let $Y$ be a scalar key performance indicator (KPI) at layer $k$. We train a quantile regression model $\hat{q}_\alpha(\mathbf{x}), \hat{q}_{1-\alpha}(\mathbf{x})$ using a deep ensemble, but then calibrate the predicted intervals via split‑conformal prediction on a held‑out calibration set $\{(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{cal}}}$. Define the conformity score

s_i = \max\{\hat{q}_\alpha(\mathbf{x}_i) - y_i,\ y_i - \hat{q}_{1-\alpha}(\mathbf{x}_i)\}.

From the calibration scores we compute the $1\!-\!\alpha$ empirical quantile $\hat{Q}_{1-\alpha}$. For a new input $\mathbf{x}^*$, the conformal prediction interval is

C(\mathbf{x}^*) = \big[\hat{q}_\alpha(\mathbf{x}^*) - \hat{Q}_{1-\alpha},\ \hat{q}_{1-\alpha}(\mathbf{x}^*) + \hat{Q}_{1-\alpha}\big].

By the exchangeability of calibration and test points, this interval satisfies

\Pr(Y^* \in C(\mathbf{x}^*)) \geq 1 - \alpha

exactly, regardless of the underlying distribution, providing a finite‑sample coverage guarantee. For multivariate outputs, we apply conformal prediction on a scalarised risk function or jointly via copula methods.

3.2 Layer‑Boundary Decision Gates with Adaptive Thresholds

The gating decision from layer $k$ to $k+1$ is based on the width of the conformal interval:

w_k(\mathbf{x}^*) = \big(\hat{q}_{1-\alpha}(\mathbf{x}^*) + \hat{Q}_{1-\alpha}\big) - \big(\hat{q}_\alpha(\mathbf{x}^*) - \hat{Q}_{1-\alpha}\big).

Instead of a fixed threshold $\delta_k$, we adapt the threshold using false‑discovery rate (FDR) control. At each layer boundary, we maintain a set of historical gate widths from successfully executed capsules and set $\delta_k$ as the $\tau$‑quantile of this distribution, with $\tau$ chosen so that the expected proportion of false gate rejections is bounded by $\alpha_{\text{FDR}}$ according to the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure. When epistemic uncertainty dominates—measured by the ratio of ensemble variance to total variance—the gate triggers human query or fidelity escalation as before.

3.3 Scalable Bayesian Inference

To reduce the computational cost of deep ensembles while maintaining calibrated uncertainty, we employ a last‑layer Laplace approximation (SWAG). Given a pre‑trained network, we fit a Gaussian posterior over the final layer’s weights using the online Newton step. The predictive distribution at inference is the marginalised Gaussian, providing both mean and closed‑form variance in $O(p)$ where $p$ is the number of last‑layer parameters. This is used for real‑time MPC state prediction and L0 surrogate Bayesian optimisation.

