8. Layer 5 — Recursive Self‑Improvement with Variational Continual Learning and First‑Order Meta‑Learning

8.1 Probabilistic World Model as a Variational Ensemble

The world model is a Bayesian neural network that predicts $p(\mathbf{s}_{t+1} \mid \mathbf{s}_t, \mathbf{a}_t)$. We replace deep ensembles and EWC with Variational Continual Learning (VCL). The model maintains a posterior over weights $q_t(\theta)$ after observing task sequences. For a new dataset $\mathcal{D}_{\text{new}}$, we minimise the variational free energy

\mathcal{L} = \mathbb{E}_{q_t(\theta)}[-\log p(\mathcal{D}_{\text{new}} \mid \theta)] + \text{KL}\big(q_t(\theta) \| q_{t-1}(\theta)\big).

The prior for the new task is set to the previous posterior, naturally regularising against catastrophic forgetting. We use a diagonal Gaussian approximation for $q_t$ with online Fisher updates to obtain a scalable implementation. The predictive distribution for a novel state is then approximated by Monte Carlo sampling from the posterior, providing both mean and epistemic uncertainty.

8.2 First‑Order Meta‑Learning

The outer meta‑learning loop optimises a set of meta‑parameters $\Theta$ (initialisation and learning rate) to maximise adaptation speed across capsule tasks. We adopt a first‑order MAML (FO‑MAML) objective:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{FO-MAML}}(\Theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau}\!\left[\mathcal{L}^\tau\!\big(\Theta - \alpha \nabla_\Theta \mathcal{L}^\tau_{\text{support}}(\Theta)\big)\right] + \lambda C(\Theta).

The inner loop uses a single gradient step (or Reptile‑style moving average). FO‑MAML avoids second‑order derivative computation, making it compatible with the VCL world model. We maintain separate meta‑parameters for the ingestion extractors, the simulation surrogates, and the controller, each adapted per capsule domain. A hypernetwork generates domain‑specific task embeddings that condition the world model and planner, allowing information sharing across domains without weight interference.

8.3 Uncertainty‑Driven Physical Micro‑Experiments

When epistemic uncertainty (measured by the variance of the VCL predictive distribution) exceeds a threshold in a state region relevant to a planned design action, the system schedules a micro‑experiment using the BALD acquisition function. The mutual information between next‑state and model parameters is

\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{a} \mid \mathbf{s}) = \frac{1}{2}\log\!\left(1 + \frac{\sigma^2_{\text{epist}}(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a})}{\sigma^2_{\text{noise}}}\right).

The experiment maximises $\mathcal{I}$ minus physical cost, subject to tier‑level safety CBFs and energy budget. The new data update the VCL posterior, and the improved world model re‑informs simulation and reasoning, closing the recursive improvement loop. The micro‑experiment loop terminates when uncertainty drops below threshold, budget is exhausted, or maximum iterations are reached.

