9. Cross‑Cutting Concerns: Capability Tiers, Governance, and Human Symbiosis

9.1 Capability Tier System with CBF Integration

The five‑level tier system $T_k = (\mathcal{A}_k, E_k^{\max}, H_k, \mathbb{C}_k)$ defines admissible actions, power limits, human oversight modes, and constitutional constraints. Tiers are partially ordered. Transitions require human approval, proof that new constraints $\mathbb{C}_{k+1} \setminus \mathbb{C}_k$ are satisfied, and a simulation‑only test at the new tier.

Each tier’s safety set is encoded both as Lean 4 theorems and as a CBF $h_{k}(\mathbf{z}) \geq 0$. The static kernel proves that $h_{k}$ is a valid CBF for the modelled dynamics and that $h_{k}(\mathbf{z}) \geq 0$ implies all constraints in $\mathbb{C}_k$. At runtime, the CBF filter enforces $h_{k}(\mathbf{z}) \geq 0$, providing a dynamic guarantee even when the true dynamics deviate from the model.

9.2 Immutable Governance Ledger

Every capsule version, reasoning step, simulation result, physical action, and sensor reading is recorded in a Merkle‑hash‑tree‑based append‑only ledger, cryptographically linked and timestamped, meeting EU AI Act and NIST AI RMF traceability requirements.

9.3 Human‑AI Scientific Symbiosis

Human researchers are first‑class agents via interfaces for ingestion disambiguation, capsule design review, tier‑transition approval, and hypothesis co‑generation. The symbiosis score $\Sigma_{\text{sym}}$ measures the fraction of capsules requiring no unscheduled human intervention; it is monitored weekly to guide continuous improvement of uncertainty estimation and extraction quality.

