Appendix B: Formal Specification of CIR and Lean 4 Kernel

B.1 Full Grammar of $\mathcal{L}_{\text{CIR}}$

\begin{aligned}
\text{Sort} &: \text{Sort } s \quad (s \in \{\text{Prop}, \text{Type}\}) \\
\text{BaseType} &: \mathbb{R} \mid \mathbb{N} \mid \mathbb{Z} \mid \text{Vec } n\ \alpha \mid \text{Matrix } m\ n\ \alpha \mid \text{MeasureSpace} \mid \text{PDEDomain} \mid \text{Dim} \\
\text{Term} &: x \mid c \mid \lambda x: \tau. t \mid t_1\ t_2 \mid \text{let } x := t_1 \text{ in } t_2 \mid \partial t/\partial x \mid \int_\Omega t\ d\mu \\
&\quad \mid \text{have } h : \phi := \text{proof} \mid t \otimes d \quad (\text{unit dimension } d) \\
\text{Proposition} &: t_1 = t_2 \mid t_1 \le t_2 \mid \forall x:\tau.\phi \mid \exists x:\tau.\phi \mid \phi_1\wedge\phi_2 \mid \phi_1\vee\phi_2 \mid \neg\phi \\
&\quad \mid \text{DimHomogeneous } t \mid \text{ConservationHolds } t\ \Omega \mid \text{CBFValid } h\ f\ g\ \alpha \\
\text{Dim} &: \text{kg}^a\cdot\text{m}^b\cdot\text{s}^c\cdot\text{K}^d\cdot\text{A}^e\cdot\text{mol}^f\cdot\text{cd}^g
\end{aligned}

Every physical quantity term must be of type $\mathbb{R}\otimes D$ for some dimension $D$. Arithmetic operators are overloaded to require dimensional consistency: $t_1 \otimes D_1 + t_2 \otimes D_2$ type-checks only if $D_1 = D_2$.

B.2 Unit-Dimension Type System

The dimension type is implemented as a type-level list of integer exponents. The dim_check tactic normalises dimensions using the Buckingham $\pi$ theorem via a canonical representation. A term expr representing a physical law is dimensionally homogeneous if its left- and right-hand sides have the same dimension, or if it equates a dimensionless quantity.

B.3 Tactic Library Specification

· dim_check: attempts to prove DimHomogeneous t by computing the dimension vector of t using a symbolic algebra of dimensions.
· conserv_check: reduces a conservation-law statement to the divergence theorem applied to a control volume, using the measure_space library.
· tier_gate_check: evaluates a tier-specific predicate (e.g., force bound) by unfolding definitions and calling linarith or nlinarith.
· cbf_check: for a given function $h$, dynamics $f,g$, and class $\mathcal{K}$ function $\alpha$, attempts to prove $\forall \mathbf{z}\in\mathcal{C},\; \nabla h^\top f + \dots + \alpha(h) \ge 0$ using derivatives and arithmetic tactics.

B.4 Constitutional Constraint Propositions per Tier

The base set $\mathbb{C}_0$ contains propositions such as:

· $\forall t,\ \|\mathbf{p}_{\text{ee}}(t)\| \le R_{\text{workspace}}$
· $\forall t,\ \|\mathbf{f}_{\text{contact}}(t)\| \le F_{\max}$
· $\forall t,\ d(\mathbf{p}_{\text{ee}}(t),\mathbf{p}_{\text{human}}) \ge d_{\min}$
· $\forall a \in \pi,\ \text{Reversible}(a) \lor \text{HumanApproved}(a)$

Higher tiers add constraints: e.g., P3 adds chemical safety flags, temperature limits for extruder, etc. All are encoded as Lean Prop and verified at plan generation.

B.5 Validation Compiler Algorithm

Algorithm B.1: ValidateCapsule($\mathcal{C}$)

1. Parse JSON-LD, check SHACL shapes; verify all hashes.
2. For each $m \in \mathcal{M}$: type-check with Lean kernel; if failure, flag.
3. For each $p \in \mathcal{P}$ with status proven, verify proof certificate; if invalid, downgrade.
4. For each $p$ with status assumed, draw $N_{\text{val}}$ samples from $\mathcal{U}$; compute empirical satisfaction rate $r$. If $r < \beta$, reject; if $\beta \le r < 1-\alpha$, flag for review; else pass.
5. Return validation report with pass/fail/review status.

